{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"a90b8518ba7abbf4","name":"card_event_scatter_tactics","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/WorldAtWar/events/card_event_scatter_tactics","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"SCATTER TACTICS","text":"When a unit attacks, give enemy units on the battlefield -2 attack until end of turn.","flavorText":"The German blitzkrieg tactics, which proved so successful during the early stages of WWII, were in fact based on their old standard tactical doctrine of a “war of maneuver” and “concentration of forces”. Although there was much focus on armored and mechanized warfare, the Wehrmacht used many aspects of their stormtrooper tactics from 1918 and only 18% of the army was mechanized in 1940-41, the rest was dependent on horse drawn wagons and carts. It is true that German tanks had a lot more radios that they used effectively and their infantry and artillery anti-tank tactics were very effective. However, tank on tank warfare was never a thing and the so-called Panzer Blitzkrieg was a myth created by the German propaganda machine. In hindsight it can be said that the German onslaught through the Ardennes was a huge gamble and could have been easily countered by competent generals. But at the time the Allied armies were ill prepared, their political and military leadership was in turmoil and they didn’t heed the warning signs of what was coming for them.","faction":"Germany","type":"gotcha","rarity":"Common","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"Name":"card_event_scatter_tactics","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_scatter_tactics_title","SourceString":"SCATTER TACTICS","LocalizedString":"SCATTER TACTICS"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::gotcha","faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_scatter_tactics_text","SourceString":"When a unit attacks, give enemy units on the battlefield -2 attack until end of turn.","LocalizedString":"When a unit attacks, give enemy units on the battlefield -2 attack until end of turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_scatter_tactics_history","SourceString":"The German blitzkrieg tactics, which proved so successful during the early stages of WWII, were in fact based on their old standard tactical doctrine of a “war of maneuver” and “concentration of forces”. Although there was much focus on armored and mechanized warfare, the Wehrmacht used many aspects of their stormtrooper tactics from 1918 and only 18% of the army was mechanized in 1940-41, the rest was dependent on horse drawn wagons and carts. It is true that German tanks had a lot more radios that they used effectively and their infantry and artillery anti-tank tactics were very effective. However, tank on tank warfare was never a thing and the so-called Panzer Blitzkrieg was a myth created by the German propaganda machine. In hindsight it can be said that the German onslaught through the Ardennes was a huge gamble and could have been easily countered by competent generals. But at the time the Allied armies were ill prepared, their political and military leadership was in turmoil and they didn’t heed the warning signs of what was coming for them.","LocalizedString":"The German blitzkrieg tactics, which proved so successful during the early stages of WWII, were in fact based on their old standard tactical doctrine of a “war of maneuver” and “concentration of forces”. Although there was much focus on armored and mechanized warfare, the Wehrmacht used many aspects of their stormtrooper tactics from 1918 and only 18% of the army was mechanized in 1940-41, the rest was dependent on horse drawn wagons and carts. It is true that German tanks had a lot more radios that they used effectively and their infantry and artillery anti-tank tactics were very effective. However, tank on tank warfare was never a thing and the so-called Panzer Blitzkrieg was a myth created by the German propaganda machine. In hindsight it can be said that the German onslaught through the Ardennes was a huge gamble and could have been easily countered by competent generals. But at the time the Allied armies were ill prepared, their political and military leadership was in turmoil and they didn’t heed the warning signs of what was coming for them."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::WorldAtWar","isReserved":true,"usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardAttacks"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Germany/CandidateArtwork/t_scatter_tactics.t_scatter_tactics","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/SCATTER_TACTICS.SCATTER_TACTICS","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":387.0},"historyPhotoAuthor":"Utecht","historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_scatter_tactics_photo","SourceString":"German horse drawn artillery, Norway 1940.","LocalizedString":"German horse drawn artillery, Norway 1940."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"OnOtherCardAttacks","owner":"card_event_scatter_tactics_C","params":[{"name":"cardAttacking","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{"name":"defenderCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{"name":"stopAttack","type":"bool","direction":"out"},{"name":"AttackedAndStopped","type":"bool","direction":"out"}],"pure":false,"event":true,"flags":"FUNC_Event | FUNC_Public | FUNC_HasOutParms | FUNC_HasDefaults | FUNC_BlueprintCallable | FUNC_BlueprintEvent","locals":[{"name":"CallFunc_IsLocatedOnBoard_isIt","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_IsUnit_isIt","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"Ca